  'Literary Theory and Criticism',
  'Literary Theory and Criticism',
  'Frame literary theory as disciplined inquiry into how texts mean, how readers interpret, and how institutions define literary value. Topics cover formalism and close reading, structuralism and narratology, reader-response and reception, Marxist criticism, psychoanalysis, feminist and queer theory, critical race and decolonial thought, postcolonial and world literature, new historicism and archives, disability and affect, ecocriticism, digital humanities, research design, and a theory-driven critical project.',
  $lthejson${"slug": "literary-theory-and-criticism", "title": "Literary Theory and Criticism", "description": "Frame literary theory as disciplined inquiry into how texts mean, how readers interpret, and how institutions define literary value. Topics cover formalism and close reading, structuralism and narratology, reader-response and reception, Marxist criticism, psychoanalysis, feminist and queer theory, critical race and decolonial thought, postcolonial and world literature, new historicism and archives, disability and affect, ecocriticism, digital humanities, research design, and a theory-driven critical project.", "topics": [{"id": "efd7451e-743b-4172-beb8-fd29cc72da8b", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory, Criticism, Method, and the Work of Interpretation", "description": "Frame literary theory as disciplined inquiry into how texts mean, how readers interpret, and how institutions define literary value.", "children": [{"id": "359f9c37-aaba-44b9-b4ec-53eb4e0e3e64", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What Theory Does", "children": [{"id": "ad3dabe8-cc0c-44ab-9431-0f0b1b5a5172", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory, criticism, method, interpretation, evaluation, and critique"}, {"id": "515aab44-d43e-4587-8c0b-095b45fc433d", "type": "concept", "title": "Text, context, author, reader, form, institution, and canon"}, {"id": "10a7efc5-d000-485e-b511-815598e16297", "type": "concept", "title": "Strong readings, weak readings, assumptions, stakes, and limits"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces theory as a way of asking better questions about literature, culture, language, value, and power."}, {"id": "b4c5b4cc-07c1-4084-894d-6c65885cdcd1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical Schools and Methodological Choice", "children": [{"id": "f7648144-2239-4aa3-a418-6aac29ac5558", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal, historical, ideological, psychoanalytic, feminist, postcolonial, digital, and environmental methods"}, {"id": "8158077e-668e-418a-a727-2cfd9fc79c57", "type": "concept", "title": "Method statements, critical vocabulary, scope, scale, and object of study"}, {"id": "21a16959-9394-46c5-a5df-41dd52f5ebb1", "type": "concept", "title": "Combining methods without flattening their differences"}], "description": "Students learn that methods are not neutral tools: each method makes some evidence visible and can obscure other evidence."}, {"id": "ae4ba159-4d53-4c87-8db8-fb11de072ea6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Institution of Literary Study", "children": [{"id": "82718dbd-842c-40d0-88d0-658edc812276", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation, anthologies, departments, journals, peer review, and professional associations"}, {"id": "4ecdc4fd-b4f5-4333-baea-386eca948cd8", "type": "concept", "title": "High culture, popular culture, curriculum, access, and disciplinary boundaries"}, {"id": "e4a49abc-579c-442f-b232-540338e281d4", "type": "concept", "title": "The public purpose of literary criticism"}], "description": "This subtopic frames theory inside universities, publishing, syllabi, prizes, archives, and public culture."}]}, {"id": "ee085324-b89b-4961-979d-2445a585e789", "type": "topic", "title": "Formalism, New Criticism, and Close Reading", "description": "Build rigorous attention to form, language, pattern, ambiguity, and textual evidence before moving outward to larger contexts.", "children": [{"id": "f781c726-5922-4a3c-a3c5-10f227962a35", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Form and Literariness", "children": [{"id": "926c64ca-aa96-42c0-8898-31890866cdc6", "type": "concept", "title": "Defamiliarization, device, pattern, repetition, foregrounding, and literariness"}, {"id": "af5fd35a-3e1e-4a8c-a397-c4d3e65d5566", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound, image, metaphor, syntax, genre, and structure"}, {"id": "3c266b41-c772-4b88-94aa-d8fda9d5c8f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Russian formalism, Prague structuralism, and modernist experiment"}], "description": "Formalism teaches students to notice how literary language is made and how form transforms ordinary perception."}, {"id": "edcee8f6-e691-43bb-9a74-c76baa38f2ef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New Critical Method", "children": [{"id": "a04a89ea-4afa-493d-8022-de38a86fbdf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, paradox, irony, tension, organic unity, and the well-wrought urn"}, {"id": "8b2789f3-e1ae-4e5a-9265-5404d1cddc5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Intentional fallacy, affective fallacy, speaker, tone, and textual autonomy"}, {"id": "e4c2a20f-4390-4537-83d6-e3bdfbf20ded", "type": "concept", "title": "Strengths and limits of text-centered interpretation"}], "description": "New Criticism models disciplined close reading, with special attention to complexity inside the text itself."}, {"id": "3f88b584-ddfa-419e-91e4-4056d743a521", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close Reading as Portable Skill", "children": [{"id": "45d5d04c-7da1-44c2-a0f5-8e21687575bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation selection, microanalysis, paraphrase, and inference"}, {"id": "ad3c5b04-ec4d-4fc1-836f-b2ca7e8ee304", "type": "concept", "title": "Form-content relation, pattern recognition, and interpretive sequence"}, {"id": "0d5db207-9969-4715-9a67-55a3c1d6f4fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Moving from observation to argument"}], "description": "Close reading remains a foundational practice because it trains students to make precise claims from textual evidence."}]}, {"id": "749b7ba1-89db-492a-b22e-510dfc455c9d", "type": "topic", "title": "Structuralism, Semiotics, and Narratology", "description": "Study meaning as a product of signs, systems, codes, genres, and narrative structures.", "children": [{"id": "29f601db-307e-442b-9cd9-02c31f0aff21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Signs and Systems", "children": [{"id": "3c049e70-a26e-44d6-b118-7e44649cbd1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Signifier, signified, sign, code, langue, parole, difference, and arbitrariness"}, {"id": "ea4f4562-68bd-47fc-96c7-ba449d7c0047", "type": "concept", "title": "Saussure, Jakobson, Levi-Strauss, Barthes, and semiotic systems"}, {"id": "08cec0c1-429e-44bd-b075-a0a6c4227c21", "type": "concept", "title": "Language as a model for culture"}], "description": "Structuralism shifts attention from individual meaning to the systems and differences that make meaning possible."}, {"id": "6a054e1d-86b8-4409-a7d8-fa551e6ad8aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narrative Structure", "children": [{"id": "87a55c70-8310-4aef-81c8-76b0a33ad351", "type": "concept", "title": "Story, discourse, plot, fabula, syuzhet, narrator, narratee, and focalization"}, {"id": "7707fbe5-65b8-44da-8ced-164989f2fbd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Order, duration, frequency, frame narrative, narrative level, and point of view"}, {"id": "5009a432-fe24-411f-87ff-9595429c67d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Genette, Todorov, Propp, Greimas, Bal, and structural poetics"}], "description": "Narratology gives students precise terms for describing how stories are organized and how narrative information moves."}, {"id": "a26c9bae-3b38-4a28-8868-5e456240f12c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genre, Convention, and Code", "children": [{"id": "5e35ff6d-9c61-4151-ae7c-d5cecfe6d9b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre conventions, motifs, binary oppositions, myth structures, and cultural grammar"}, {"id": "ae8b680c-a991-4c20-9d23-b953df744116", "type": "concept", "title": "Poetics, taxonomy, rules, transformation, and transgression"}, {"id": "d2bfed50-1559-4ea9-a5e6-badedf6f6e46", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of abstraction and universalism"}], "description": "This subtopic helps students see genre as a system of expectations rather than a simple label."}]}, {"id": "5dd81ab5-baf9-46c7-bda6-e59bae7b3a35", "type": "topic", "title": "Reader-Response, Reception Theory, and Interpretive Communities", "description": "Ask how readers, publics, institutions, and historical horizons participate in producing literary meaning.", "children": [{"id": "0d57d05c-f75c-41ad-88a0-44fe2455d989", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Active Reader", "children": [{"id": "c7ea17da-0ab2-4c55-bc07-fb377992a89d", "type": "concept", "title": "Implied reader, gaps, concretization, response, anticipation, and retrospection"}, {"id": "a7160ea4-3a90-4696-9d08-88f56d05d62c", "type": "concept", "title": "Iser, Fish, Rosenblatt, Holland, and readerly experience"}, {"id": "3beb0abe-b7e2-4cc8-8c10-d13751599a71", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading as process rather than finished product"}], "description": "Reader-response theory asks how meaning happens in the encounter between text and reader."}, {"id": "95ee6a8a-5504-4da5-ae72-ab33fc71ff90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reception and Historical Horizons", "children": [{"id": "2ed9d325-2d66-4bd9-b72e-356a4d9a4aca", "type": "concept", "title": "Horizon of expectations, reception history, reviews, adaptation, translation, and canonization"}, {"id": "984d7e62-d56e-4b8b-8310-d5e8483b4d0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Jauss, Gadamer, hermeneutics, tradition, and historical distance"}, {"id": "cfdf15b5-fa26-421d-ba60-0c70bc55f39d", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence from reviews, prefaces, syllabi, archives, and readership studies"}], "description": "Reception theory studies how texts change meaning across time, publics, editions, classrooms, and cultural moments."}, {"id": "4cbe6a96-ad77-4266-b012-e28e587aa32b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive Communities", "children": [{"id": "8e93be12-4023-40bc-b840-0c7c3b5ce344", "type": "concept", "title": "Classrooms, disciplines, publics, fandoms, book clubs, and online communities"}, {"id": "67ac3efb-a99d-4b62-b7a4-7a82b0a97768", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority, norms, disagreement, expertise, and interpretive rules"}, {"id": "c200d6f7-1009-49ed-b5c1-b0a8408ff562", "type": "concept", "title": "How communities authorize some readings and dismiss others"}], "description": "Students learn that interpretation is social, institutional, and trained, not merely private opinion."}]}, {"id": "93af7cde-87ae-42e6-8863-d7570c1ebc52", "type": "topic", "title": "Marxist Criticism, Ideology, and Cultural Materialism", "description": "Analyze literature in relation to class, labor, capitalism, ideology, cultural production, and historical contradiction.", "children": [{"id": "3b2f66f1-50bd-4bd1-9096-28ef965d3bed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literature and Material History", "children": [{"id": "4ccd6749-6d67-4182-a3ab-25b4375eb761", "type": "concept", "title": "Base and superstructure, mode of production, class struggle, commodity, and reification"}, {"id": "46434d97-6a61-4c48-a24f-568bef87c11b", "type": "concept", "title": "Realism, modernism, totality, mediation, and contradiction"}, {"id": "98b9c860-540a-4588-9fde-32604225df71", "type": "concept", "title": "Marx, Engels, Lukacs, Brecht, Benjamin, and Jameson"}], "description": "Marxist criticism links literary form to labor, class, production, property, and historical change."}, {"id": "155778fe-8818-4141-a90c-8ef61747f352", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology and Hegemony", "children": [{"id": "e3b01243-f86a-401c-84e3-240b840eaff6", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology, interpellation, hegemony, common sense, false consciousness, and cultural consent"}, {"id": "c2c6f983-9871-42c7-b2bb-cb7f8424ec01", "type": "concept", "title": "Althusser, Gramsci, Williams, Hall, and cultural materialism"}, {"id": "87450e40-4d28-4b20-b0b9-9333473650d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading form as social evidence"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how literature can reproduce, expose, or contest dominant social assumptions."}, {"id": "c8d37de4-8e3d-40bb-99db-2d85b2b726b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture, Market, and Institution", "children": [{"id": "97a349fb-b42c-42f2-b517-85e1bbf680a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship as labor, literary marketplace, prizes, reviews, and cultural capital"}, {"id": "5e40a71b-4449-4228-89cb-1d67b1b5add8", "type": "concept", "title": "High culture, popular culture, mass culture, and the culture industry"}, {"id": "7212f441-4757-4335-86b1-e9f62db7dd7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Political criticism without reducing texts to slogans"}], "description": "Students analyze literature as cultural production shaped by publishers, audiences, labor, media, and value systems."}]}, {"id": "3c12b18d-329d-4be2-a218-79a6bf026984", "type": "topic", "title": "Psychoanalysis, Desire, and the Textual Unconscious", "description": "Use psychoanalytic concepts to read desire, fantasy, repression, subject formation, and symbolic structure in texts.", "children": [{"id": "b6e7d3fd-d7c5-4901-a731-8f1d7f228c4f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freudian Reading", "children": [{"id": "e4f59595-28bd-4da3-802f-c8298d272b40", "type": "concept", "title": "Unconscious, dream work, condensation, displacement, repression, symptom, and repetition"}, {"id": "5b4b5b98-b214-41af-a605-debeac66c941", "type": "concept", "title": "Oedipus complex, narcissism, mourning, melancholia, and the uncanny"}, {"id": "fe46b63c-d5e0-4801-bc38-ce2236350919", "type": "concept", "title": "Author, character, reader, and textual unconscious"}], "description": "Freudian criticism treats texts as scenes where desire, repression, fantasy, and conflict become legible."}, {"id": "41f31db9-bd72-4fd9-b11b-1f9d2350a57f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lacanian Theory", "children": [{"id": "7d69005e-0ca5-4d6d-bf8a-bb0ec60ad868", "type": "concept", "title": "Imaginary, Symbolic, Real, mirror stage, objet petit a, lack, and signifying chain"}, {"id": "c4380673-d67b-4551-82a5-75029420def1", "type": "concept", "title": "Language, subjectivity, desire, fantasy, and the Other"}, {"id": "84d254d4-f8f3-46cb-a920-bfcfe77cf238", "type": "concept", "title": "Why meaning and identity remain unstable"}], "description": "Lacan reframes psychoanalysis through language, lack, symbolic order, desire, and subject formation."}, {"id": "c631c89a-55ca-4087-af2c-5efb7c4189f4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics and Limits of Psychoanalytic Method", "children": [{"id": "9e6dade5-8974-4224-a106-b11a899e3680", "type": "concept", "title": "Overinterpretation, pathologizing, evidence, and historical specificity"}, {"id": "6f18c2ab-b95a-4835-be1e-28cb06991ba4", "type": "concept", "title": "Psychoanalysis with feminism, queer theory, race theory, and trauma studies"}, {"id": "247f7e58-4c44-4db3-84d3-21732d0eaf89", "type": "concept", "title": "Desire as structure, not just biographical content"}], "description": "Students learn to use psychoanalytic reading carefully, avoiding crude diagnosis while preserving its interpretive force."}]}, {"id": "4c9e1b27-db77-4dcc-8a54-eb402f2e8a7c", "type": "topic", "title": "Feminist Criticism, Gender Theory, and Queer Theory", "description": "Examine how literature constructs, contests, and reimagines gender, sexuality, embodiment, authorship, and social norms.", "children": [{"id": "9fbd322c-34a1-4b71-a2f0-b7e3e233969e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist Literary Criticism", "children": [{"id": "8fa8471a-58d1-4d11-a675-d565241f4eb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Recovery work, gynocriticism, patriarchy, domesticity, authorship, and feminist narratology"}, {"id": "c68dbb9e-b239-4800-93dc-67d51d892625", "type": "concept", "title": "Woolf, Beauvoir, Showalter, Gilbert and Gubar, Cixous, hooks, and Spivak"}, {"id": "dfbbb8aa-d57f-42fd-bfc5-c4372e8334a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon revision, literary value, and institutional critique"}], "description": "Feminist criticism transforms questions of authorship, canon, voice, genre, labor, body, and representation."}, {"id": "794d84e0-7616-4c57-b85c-01368803b31d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and Performativity", "children": [{"id": "8666d551-feed-4e56-b0f3-a0070242994d", "type": "concept", "title": "Performativity, embodiment, masculinity, femininity, trans studies, and gender systems"}, {"id": "22ac9cd8-e7af-4851-8418-8611620a7592", "type": "concept", "title": "Butler, Sedgwick, Halberstam, Ahmed, and intersectional feminism"}, {"id": "ce551072-8ede-424b-90fa-57e878ca258f", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender as form, plot, voice, and reading practice"}], "description": "Gender theory teaches students to read gender as historical, embodied, linguistic, and socially performed."}, {"id": "58aa8013-bbbe-449c-a282-3308371c05b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer Reading Practices", "children": [{"id": "245fe182-8a43-4209-9fa6-82da9020447a", "type": "concept", "title": "Heteronormativity, closet, camp, queer temporality, reparative reading, and kinship"}, {"id": "0ad608f1-d29a-4fde-a026-f37b4307ab87", "type": "concept", "title": "Sedgwick, Foucault, Berlant, Munoz, Warner, and Jagose"}, {"id": "6d7ea107-7e1e-49d5-976d-279e7cd5924c", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading for silence, coded meaning, refusal, and alternative social worlds"}], "description": "Queer theory examines sexuality, normativity, kinship, temporality, secrecy, desire, and resistant interpretation."}]}, {"id": "d4c0af11-ef2b-4871-8a62-49e5e012e697", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Race Theory, Black Studies, Ethnic Studies, and Decolonial Thought", "description": "Read texts through race, coloniality, migration, racial formation, canon politics, and the conditions of literary knowledge.", "children": [{"id": "432d3e78-2c7a-4e33-be78-92173914ca63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and Literary Form", "children": [{"id": "246cffb6-ab34-41c6-b09b-23dd1e204f4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial formation, racial capitalism, double consciousness, passing, diaspora, and fugitivity"}, {"id": "f7cca5da-90d6-49a9-8d60-b5e9e2940e7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois, Morrison, Gates, Hartman, Gilroy, Wynter, and Moten"}, {"id": "8be631cf-7515-4863-acef-bf70925f27cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Race as structure, aesthetic problem, and historical force"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how racial formation shapes genre, narrative voice, symbolism, character, and canon."}, {"id": "1b36be0a-2a66-464f-a255-5e5b2e045fd3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnic Studies and Comparative Racialization", "children": [{"id": "eb97de2c-655f-4ecd-b8dc-7b1b55064608", "type": "concept", "title": "Asian American, Latinx, Indigenous, Arab American, and comparative ethnic literary studies"}, {"id": "7ec59c0c-efb1-415a-a0e8-eb242def0b62", "type": "concept", "title": "Immigration, settler colonialism, language politics, borderlands, sovereignty, and memory"}, {"id": "d9a3b774-053c-427a-8bff-e4ad6643e024", "type": "concept", "title": "Community archives, oral histories, and canon expansion"}], "description": "Students learn to read literature across migration, language, ethnicity, nation, and uneven citizenship."}, {"id": "b326f12f-3ebc-46a0-b8c1-5e59b4b96a5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decolonial and Institutional Critique", "children": [{"id": "8e632bc9-6028-46db-aab5-2c3ebaa9cb86", "type": "concept", "title": "Coloniality, epistemic violence, decolonization, extraction, settler colonialism, and sovereignty"}, {"id": "d0000388-acc5-4ea0-a21c-656c3511bb2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Fanon, Anzaldua, Lorde, Lugones, Mignolo, Simpson, and Tuck and Yang"}, {"id": "4e5762f0-1db4-470a-9112-49bdc16a8486", "type": "concept", "title": "The ethics of citation, archive, syllabus, and scholarly authority"}], "description": "Decolonial thought examines how knowledge, curriculum, archives, and literary value inherit colonial power."}]}, {"id": "b2bc236d-3314-4932-89e7-f7115af178f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Postcolonial Theory, Global Anglophone Critique, and World Literature", "description": "Connect literature to empire, language, translation, diaspora, global circulation, and uneven cultural power.", "children": [{"id": "6a44f2c2-d3bd-46c1-a2f1-846c26915b40", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial Discourse and Representation", "children": [{"id": "1c97670c-c79e-447d-b6a5-4c52cd4eec07", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism, colonial discourse, mimicry, hybridity, ambivalence, and othering"}, {"id": "bb4d61af-cc5a-42cd-9a84-3ef1ca188be5", "type": "concept", "title": "Said, Bhabha, Spivak, Fanon, Cesaire, Achebe, and Ngugi wa Thiong'o"}, {"id": "d6c4ce9c-5873-4066-95a3-b4088c52896d", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading empire in language, genre, education, and narrative perspective"}], "description": "Postcolonial theory studies how empire produces knowledge, subjects, stereotypes, and literary forms."}, {"id": "de901652-eb0f-4911-9aa0-5ac9db412abd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Anglophone and Diaspora", "children": [{"id": "fba29e6a-825f-4cf3-956c-6b20c6e0986b", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora, creolization, multilingualism, cosmopolitanism, exile, and transnational publics"}, {"id": "f9654898-2d81-475e-bfcc-12316bff8c50", "type": "concept", "title": "Language choice, vernacular, code-switching, translation, and market circulation"}, {"id": "3b0f0694-e9a2-47f2-84db-25a987977d17", "type": "concept", "title": "Global Anglophone critique beyond national literary containers"}], "description": "This subtopic focuses on English-language literatures shaped by migration, diaspora, translation, and colonial histories."}, {"id": "e6849a9a-0124-4ee6-9d3f-3d26f651d214", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World Literature and Uneven Circulation", "children": [{"id": "afc08d77-1587-442a-a7ca-eac170ba3911", "type": "concept", "title": "World literary systems, center and periphery, translation, prizes, publishers, and classroom anthologies"}, {"id": "1d7a0290-ac19-47e2-870c-459607d0d6a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Damrosch, Moretti, Casanova, Apter, and debates over untranslatability"}, {"id": "ea45af79-a3a3-496d-884c-9e55b8f3dc5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative method, scale, and the politics of global reading"}], "description": "Students evaluate world literature as both an expanded field and a system shaped by unequal institutions."}]}, {"id": "f3bccc50-ab84-487b-8ebe-650685ca2bdf", "type": "topic", "title": "New Historicism, Cultural Studies, and Archive-Based Methods", "description": "Develop historically situated readings that connect literary form to archives, institutions, discourse, media, and everyday culture.", "children": [{"id": "2ae5ef1b-6e53-4336-b570-d4ddda226325", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New Historicism and Cultural Materialism", "children": [{"id": "e22d03d8-f9dc-405e-8ec0-a4deec74d56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description, anecdote, circulation of social energy, discourse, and ideology"}, {"id": "738653ff-d6dd-460b-9d3f-a983e38ecdfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Greenblatt, Gallagher, Montrose, Sinfield, Dollimore, and Renaissance studies"}, {"id": "e6e8ae7d-40ab-4148-8bf8-019a197528de", "type": "concept", "title": "Text-context relations and the risk of historical reduction"}], "description": "Historicist methods connect literary texts to power, discourse, institutions, and adjacent cultural documents."}, {"id": "86cc5a93-f807-4ebe-a226-15aa800fd061", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cultural Studies and Media Culture", "children": [{"id": "8cdbcea1-1fa2-45c9-9900-ae580c050935", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony, subculture, encoding/decoding, audience, class, race, gender, and media"}, {"id": "623c7da3-d445-4039-a543-2dfeb80532ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Birmingham School, Hall, Williams, Hoggart, Hebdige, and Grossberg"}, {"id": "f350bd86-a743-4fee-8f7b-5c9a98591fa2", "type": "concept", "title": "High culture, popular culture, and canon critique"}], "description": "Cultural studies expands literary criticism toward popular culture, media, publics, and everyday life."}, {"id": "8a12eba5-64a1-43f2-9600-f91c3772aaaf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archive and Material Text", "children": [{"id": "f4b77c98-69e9-437f-b5a1-16dee7c94b86", "type": "concept", "title": "Bibliography, textual variants, editions, paratexts, periodicals, marginalia, and publishing history"}, {"id": "e97b1dff-2d53-4edb-801b-cfd4916f6b8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Special collections, finding aids, provenance, digitization, and metadata"}, {"id": "ea608a3c-f0ea-4ca7-a4b5-1b671f1c86fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Material evidence as interpretive evidence"}], "description": "Archive-based methods show how meaning depends on editions, manuscripts, paratexts, print networks, and preservation."}]}, {"id": "6c17bdfb-fb45-4fd7-b804-0221c9109249", "type": "topic", "title": "Disability Studies, Affect Theory, and Embodiment", "description": "Study how texts shape and are shaped by bodily norms, emotion, access, vulnerability, sensation, and social life.", "children": [{"id": "b7584080-2e4d-4a4c-89aa-d7a490f977bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Disability Studies and Crip Theory", "children": [{"id": "bca4a871-780d-4cc0-b540-d8ce83356919", "type": "concept", "title": "Medical model, social model, normate, cure, impairment, access, dependency, and crip time"}, {"id": "31f01347-bbec-4e5e-a02f-e7f5b1833334", "type": "concept", "title": "Garland-Thomson, Mitchell and Snyder, McRuer, Kafer, and disability aesthetics"}, {"id": "d2d6eb60-59ef-4072-a1be-ced1908ab326", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading stigma, access, embodiment, and narrative prosthesis"}], "description": "Disability studies asks how literature produces, contests, and aestheticizes norms of body, mind, access, and capacity."}, {"id": "953b1a10-5510-4f10-9d90-91bb349e4f13", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Affect, Emotion, and Public Feeling", "children": [{"id": "996ab4c4-1dfd-4f56-842e-bf93d94da9e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Affect, emotion, mood, atmosphere, attachment, shame, optimism, and public feeling"}, {"id": "58456eb0-1f8d-4131-8072-0b0e0fa76321", "type": "concept", "title": "Sedgwick, Berlant, Ahmed, Ngai, Cvetkovich, and affective economies"}, {"id": "6ad9ac52-38e9-48c5-9da0-837fed518f32", "type": "concept", "title": "How feeling organizes interpretation and social worlds"}], "description": "Affect theory gives students language for moods, intensities, attachments, shame, grief, anger, and ordinary feeling."}, {"id": "57ae4c69-c7f2-4898-a5ff-3c2e5d375d4d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodied Reading", "children": [{"id": "7634f74c-2733-494d-a7cd-b42fa8fea368", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment, phenomenology, trauma, care, illness narratives, and sensory form"}, {"id": "4c05c530-417e-4397-b3ae-df3b0c4b3831", "type": "concept", "title": "Body as theme, method, archive, and reading condition"}, {"id": "cc29f498-c403-46ce-9a8b-247c07b8ab69", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of reading bodies, pain, testimony, and difference"}], "description": "This subtopic connects interpretation to sensation, voice, performance, attention, memory, and vulnerability."}]}, {"id": "4133038f-42c5-41a9-9793-fb6dc027788c", "type": "topic", "title": "Ecocriticism, Environmental Humanities, and More-than-Human Studies", "description": "Rethink literary scale through climate, land, extraction, environmental justice, energy, and nonhuman agencies.", "children": [{"id": "a83af5b2-7c28-4c24-b992-630442d4be5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecocriticism and Environmental Form", "children": [{"id": "3d5a0684-b515-4566-9fb2-4e942e54456f", "type": "concept", "title": "Nature writing, pastoral, wilderness, climate fiction, toxicity, and environmental imagination"}, {"id": "b6e6eac1-d9f8-476f-b892-5b18ee795a43", "type": "concept", "title": "Buell, Glotfelty, Heise, Garrard, Nixon, and environmental justice criticism"}, {"id": "c8af9e51-c097-40c2-b87a-d5c8e93106b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale, place, slow violence, and narrative form"}], "description": "Ecocriticism asks how literary form mediates land, climate, place, scale, catastrophe, and ecological relation."}, {"id": "0ae2baeb-c000-4dfe-99a5-ce6cac305d60", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Energy, Extraction, and Environmental Justice", "children": [{"id": "2773a864-ca81-4960-a92d-b183c36622bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy humanities, extractive capitalism, petrofiction, toxicity, waste, sacrifice zones, and climate inequality"}, {"id": "b727b0c5-f319-418a-8ef4-bd2d339f8a83", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous ecological knowledge, sovereignty, land, water, and environmental memory"}, {"id": "851e0373-46ed-4278-b7a0-e175595cdca5", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading environmental crisis through power and history"}], "description": "Students learn to connect literature to infrastructure, fuel, waste, labor, colonialism, and unequal exposure."}, {"id": "d03ead6c-63fb-409d-9ce8-6ead9b12b876", "type": "subtopic", "title": "More-than-Human and Planetary Thought", "children": [{"id": "1367aa2d-f839-4a62-b8fa-85f77c17838a", "type": "concept", "title": "Anthropocene debates, planetary scale, multispecies relations, material ecocriticism, and posthumanism"}, {"id": "0f5bab53-8f6f-408e-b64d-e253d94f4bdf", "type": "concept", "title": "Haraway, Chakrabarty, Latour, Tsing, Iovino, Oppermann, and new materialism"}, {"id": "ca7fc38a-5527-4349-aa85-1eacbb3bafe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Agency, responsibility, form, and the limits of human exceptionalism"}], "description": "This subtopic expands interpretation beyond human-centered assumptions while preserving ethical and historical specificity."}]}, {"id": "d1b8db53-30cc-4cdc-bda0-cfaafebc2b0a", "type": "topic", "title": "Digital Humanities, Media Theory, and Distant Reading", "description": "Evaluate computational and media-based approaches to literature while preserving critical reflection about evidence, scale, and interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "9885c94e-db34-447b-bd32-35d143efeb19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital Humanities Debates", "children": [{"id": "c745a192-0a52-49ed-810d-b9fa458a6212", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital archives, databases, metadata, editions, TEI, public humanities, and infrastructure"}, {"id": "46d24c5c-8930-4b6c-853d-728f224195e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Drucker, Hayles, Liu, Ramsay, Gold, Klein, and critical digital humanities"}, {"id": "0903d7d1-19f6-48b7-8bc0-da21bef60f00", "type": "concept", "title": "Tool critique, access, labor, bias, and preservation"}], "description": "Digital humanities changes the scale and form of literary evidence, but it also requires critical attention to tools and data."}, {"id": "599466d3-c9ce-462e-8194-adfd455598ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Distant Reading and Textual Data", "children": [{"id": "8d503baa-3248-4867-ba4f-bae91c02d359", "type": "concept", "title": "Corpus building, text mining, topic modeling, stylometry, network analysis, mapping, and visualization"}, {"id": "cb6b4359-405d-4976-9f07-c6149d588630", "type": "concept", "title": "Moretti, Jockers, Underwood, Piper, and computational literary study"}, {"id": "871ed3ff-2586-44b7-9077-4b2f4bea40e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling, cleaning, interpretation, error, and reproducibility"}], "description": "Distant reading asks what patterns appear when students study many texts at once."}, {"id": "eb354664-d7a0-4172-8c12-195aeef87bf5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media Theory and Platform Culture", "children": [{"id": "fa88c99b-a60a-4458-815c-125d9c85e1b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Remediation, medium specificity, interface, algorithm, platform, attention, and circulation"}, {"id": "42869506-fb43-492d-bd0c-fbaaebb8e6ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Book history, media archaeology, electronic literature, fan platforms, and social reading"}, {"id": "5aace730-f9ff-4904-a9cb-9d0d4308f853", "type": "concept", "title": "How media forms shape literary value and interpretation"}], "description": "This subtopic situates literature among print, screen, audio, networked platforms, and changing reading technologies."}]}, {"id": "b8f96ea0-84e7-4c12-8828-ec51f9d9555d", "type": "topic", "title": "Method in Practice: Research Design, Bibliography, and Scholarly Intervention", "description": "Turn theory into a defensible research method with sources, stakes, evidence, and a clear scholarly contribution.", "children": [{"id": "2dab0119-45ca-4a38-b133-91bc3413458e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research Questions and Stakes", "children": [{"id": "5a80ff23-6061-49b5-b03d-cd239dd903b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Problem, puzzle, archive, corpus, primary text, scope, and scholarly audience"}, {"id": "478afa27-b7b6-4346-886e-c9eb19139796", "type": "concept", "title": "Why this question matters now and what it changes"}, {"id": "993ca63a-f48c-4d6c-9bf9-b17da236029c", "type": "concept", "title": "Feasible, original, ethical, and evidence-driven design"}], "description": "Students turn theoretical interest into researchable questions with clear stakes and a defined object of study."}, {"id": "1c3eb2f1-7eaf-480d-8984-95cab27a0caa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bibliography and Source Ecology", "children": [{"id": "8e79571c-d41c-4868-8ff0-97d5b6fc7204", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA International Bibliography, JSTOR, Project MUSE, library catalogs, Oxford Bibliographies, and finding aids"}, {"id": "87a2374f-8e3e-4bca-bca3-993cb55ef1c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography, literature review, citation chaining, and source evaluation"}, {"id": "9ecdf158-55b2-46eb-a9be-281681676db5", "type": "concept", "title": "Editions, translations, paratexts, manuscripts, and textual variants"}], "description": "World-class literary criticism depends on knowing the difference between primary texts, theoretical sources, field scholarship, and archives."}, {"id": "3b8e3df8-c170-40e8-9f76-e8f6a31105cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scholarly Intervention", "children": [{"id": "5506fff4-8cf4-4279-ac1b-4279a7c98b14", "type": "concept", "title": "Claim, evidence, method, counterargument, contribution, and revision"}, {"id": "fee9b4f2-3246-42ef-b9a2-03c4ea6ae440", "type": "concept", "title": "Method statement, theoretical framework, archive rationale, and chapter or paper structure"}, {"id": "6692ad19-81af-40cc-a732-fea7a2409d97", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible citation, peer workshop, and conference abstract"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to enter a conversation rather than merely apply terminology to a text."}]}, {"id": "bf0a01d5-339c-42f8-a1dd-e0f49a2adb38", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory-Driven Critical Project Capstone", "description": "Synthesize multiple critical traditions into an original, ethically aware, research-grounded literary argument.", "children": [{"id": "5586a59f-7e61-4388-be3c-8d0b9a0c1d08", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project Architecture", "children": [{"id": "585d479d-0478-4305-803b-7de60b236033", "type": "concept", "title": "Research prospectus, abstract, thesis, method statement, and working bibliography"}, {"id": "8bbfd814-6630-4034-872a-eaa2f1254ddb", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary corpus, theoretical framework, archive, and critical conversation"}, {"id": "af93ab06-332f-4fdc-9033-db823a69c3dd", "type": "concept", "title": "A project small enough to finish and substantial enough to matter"}], "description": "The capstone asks students to design an argument where theory shapes the research question, evidence, and reading practice."}, {"id": "de8369f2-5b3a-4436-abfc-182a1fd5e29b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative Method and Synthesis", "children": [{"id": "dd94e2af-be70-4239-8160-8eb16e31ec6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading plus context, ideology plus form, archive plus theory, digital plus interpretive analysis"}, {"id": "68b20f73-4b99-4ade-893b-f7c33ae95728", "type": "concept", "title": "Methodological tension, compatibility, contradiction, and reflective justification"}, {"id": "3d136af1-0e74-4ff3-8839-46ad8617df85", "type": "concept", "title": "Synthesis that clarifies rather than dilutes the argument"}], "description": "Students practice bringing methods into productive relation without turning theory into a list of buzzwords."}, {"id": "ee8da700-6db9-4c3b-ad7b-feaf38c8f5fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Presentation, Revision, and Public Value", "children": [{"id": "64e69900-d125-4453-b64f-c3462d17617e", "type": "concept", "title": "Seminar paper, conference presentation, annotated edition, digital exhibit, or public essay"}, {"id": "f05c79c3-7228-4ee2-b717-418c1b56b0f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision memo, peer response, oral presentation, and research poster"}, {"id": "c73e0c27-dc32-48dd-9291-2bb101dcc862", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics, accessibility, audience, and the future of literary study"}], "description": "The course ends by helping students communicate criticism as polished scholarship and as public intellectual work."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$lthejson$::jsonb,
